{"duration_api_ms":54272,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"9ea42294-c487-48f9-84c7-4dc7e73904be","total_cost_usd":0.147134,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20303,"cache_read_input_tokens":24860,"output_tokens":5257,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1099},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20303,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2098,"cache_read_input_tokens":20931,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3301},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":8292,"outputTokens":16,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.008372000000000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":5257,"cacheReadInputTokens":24860,"cacheCreationInputTokens":20303,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.138762,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1099,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"summary\":\"The executor timed out after 2100s (executor_timed_out) before completing the PostgreSQL TLS replication setup. Package installation, CA distribution, and TLS certificate/config staging succeeded on all three nodes, but the last observed pg_hba.conf on node1 still only had the default scram-sha-256 rules with no TLS/client-certificate enforcement for replication, and no evidence of pg_hba edits, service restarts, or standby bootstrapping (pg_basebackup, standby.signal, primary_conninfo) appears in the selected record set before the timeout.\",\"evidence\":[{\"source\":\"executor command audit, node1/node2/node3 apt-get install commands\",\"finding\":\"Each apt-get install call exceeded the platform's 120s proxy read timeout and was reported as 'failed', even though the install had actually completed on the host (node1's dpkg -l shows postgresql-16 fully installed with post-bootstrap init messages).\"},{\"source\":\"executor command audit, node2/node3 polling commands\",\"finding\":\"After the apt 'failure', the executor had to run explicit 100s polling loops on node2 and node3 to wait for the background apt-get process to finish, adding further wall-clock time before it could confirm postgresql-16 was installed.\"},{\"source\":\"executor command audit, CA/cert distribution steps\",\"finding\":\"A CA tarball (pgca.tgz) was built and served via a temporary HTTP server, downloaded successfully by node2 and node3, and TLS material (ca.crt, server.crt/key, replicator.crt/key) was copied into /etc/postgresql/16/main/tls on all three nodes with correct ownership/permissions.\"},{\"source\":\"executor command audit, postgresql.conf/conf.d edits\",\"finding\":\"An identical conf.d/tls_repl.conf enabling ssl, ssl_cert_file/ssl_key_file/ssl_ca_file, wal_level=replica, max_wal_senders, and hot_standby was written on all three nodes.\"},{\"source\":\"executor command audit, last pg_hba.conf check on node1\",\"finding\":\"pg_hba.conf on node1 at the time of this check still contained only the default local/peer and scram-sha-256 rules (including for the 'replication' database) — no hostssl rule requiring a client certificate from the new CA had been added yet.\"},{\"source\":\"audit selection metadata\",\"finding\":\"44 of 62 terminal command records were omitted from the redacted set (only up to 20 failures + 12 successes retained), so it cannot be confirmed with certainty that no further progress happened after the last shown pg_hba.conf check; absence of later evidence is not proof of no later action.\"}],\"likely_causes\":[{\"cause\":\"Cumulative time lost to the apt-get install step (three sequential ~125s proxy-timeout 'failures' plus two 100s polling waits, roughly 8-9 minutes total) consumed a disproportionate share of the 35-minute execution budget before any TLS/replication configuration began.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"Per-node sequential execution (running the same install/config command three times, once per node, rather than in parallel) multiplied the time cost of every slow step across the whole session.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"The task was not finished when the timeout hit: pg_hba.conf had not yet been updated to require TLS + client certificates from the new CA for replication connections, and no evidence of PostgreSQL restart, standby pg_basebackup, or primary_conninfo/standby.signal configuration was present in the visible records, suggesting the executor ran out of time before reaching the replication-bootstrap phase.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"The apt-get 'failed' outcome was a proxy/tool artifact (120s Proxy Read Timeout) rather than an actual package-manager failure, but the executor may have spent additional turns investigating/retrying under the mistaken belief that the install itself had failed.\",\"confidence\":\"low\"}],\"next_attempt_guidance\":[\"Run apt-get install in the background with output redirected to a log file and a PID file, then poll with short exec calls, instead of a single foreground call — this avoids hitting the platform's 120s proxy read timeout on all three nodes and prevents wasted retries/investigation.\",\"Where nodes have no dependency on each other (e.g. installing postgresql, copying the CA bundle, writing conf.d/tls_repl.conf), issue the exec calls for node1/node2/node3 back-to-back without waiting on each one sequentially, to reduce total wall-clock time within the fixed session budget.\",\"After TLS material and conf.d changes are in place, immediately add explicit hostssl pg_hba.conf rules for the replication database requiring clientcert=verify-full tied to the new CA (e.g. `hostssl replication replicator 0.0.0.0/0 cert clientcert=verify-full`) before moving on — do this early rather than leaving it for a later pass, since it is central to the task requirement of accepting replication only from CA-issued certs.\",\"Verify actual package/service state with idempotent checks (e.g. `dpkg -l | grep postgresql-16`, `systemctl is-active postgresql`) rather than trusting a tool-reported timeout as a functional failure, to avoid unnecessary re-work.\",\"Reserve time explicitly for the replica bootstrap phase (pg_basebackup from node1, standby.signal or primary_conninfo with sslmode=verify-full and sslcert/sslkey pointing at the replicator certificate, postgresql restart) since this phase had not started by the time of the last observed evidence — consider doing a time-budget check partway through the session and prioritizing this phase if time is short.\",\"Do not assume the old cluster's partial state is reusable; start fresh and re-run the full sequence (CA creation, cert distribution, postgresql.conf/pg_hba.conf edits, restart, replica bootstrap, verification with `SELECT * FROM pg_stat_replication` on node1) end-to-end on the new cluster.\"]}","structured_output":{"summary":"The executor timed out after 2100s (executor_timed_out) before completing the PostgreSQL TLS replication setup. Package installation, CA distribution, and TLS certificate/config staging succeeded on all three nodes, but the last observed pg_hba.conf on node1 still only had the default scram-sha-256 rules with no TLS/client-certificate enforcement for replication, and no evidence of pg_hba edits, service restarts, or standby bootstrapping (pg_basebackup, standby.signal, primary_conninfo) appears in the selected record set before the timeout.","evidence":[{"source":"executor command audit, node1/node2/node3 apt-get install commands","finding":"Each apt-get install call exceeded the platform's 120s proxy read timeout and was reported as 'failed', even though the install had actually completed on the host (node1's dpkg -l shows postgresql-16 fully installed with post-bootstrap init messages)."},{"source":"executor command audit, node2/node3 polling commands","finding":"After the apt 'failure', the executor had to run explicit 100s polling loops on node2 and node3 to wait for the background apt-get process to finish, adding further wall-clock time before it could confirm postgresql-16 was installed."},{"source":"executor command audit, CA/cert distribution steps","finding":"A CA tarball (pgca.tgz) was built and served via a temporary HTTP server, downloaded successfully by node2 and node3, and TLS material (ca.crt, server.crt/key, replicator.crt/key) was copied into /etc/postgresql/16/main/tls on all three nodes with correct ownership/permissions."},{"source":"executor command audit, postgresql.conf/conf.d edits","finding":"An identical conf.d/tls_repl.conf enabling ssl, ssl_cert_file/ssl_key_file/ssl_ca_file, wal_level=replica, max_wal_senders, and hot_standby was written on all three nodes."},{"source":"executor command audit, last pg_hba.conf check on node1","finding":"pg_hba.conf on node1 at the time of this check still contained only the default local/peer and scram-sha-256 rules (including for the 'replication' database) — no hostssl rule requiring a client certificate from the new CA had been added yet."},{"source":"audit selection metadata","finding":"44 of 62 terminal command records were omitted from the redacted set (only up to 20 failures + 12 successes retained), so it cannot be confirmed with certainty that no further progress happened after the last shown pg_hba.conf check; absence of later evidence is not proof of no later action."}],"likely_causes":[{"cause":"Cumulative time lost to the apt-get install step (three sequential ~125s proxy-timeout 'failures' plus two 100s polling waits, roughly 8-9 minutes total) consumed a disproportionate share of the 35-minute execution budget before any TLS/replication configuration began.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"Per-node sequential execution (running the same install/config command three times, once per node, rather than in parallel) multiplied the time cost of every slow step across the whole session.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"The task was not finished when the timeout hit: pg_hba.conf had not yet been updated to require TLS + client certificates from the new CA for replication connections, and no evidence of PostgreSQL restart, standby pg_basebackup, or primary_conninfo/standby.signal configuration was present in the visible records, suggesting the executor ran out of time before reaching the replication-bootstrap phase.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"The apt-get 'failed' outcome was a proxy/tool artifact (120s Proxy Read Timeout) rather than an actual package-manager failure, but the executor may have spent additional turns investigating/retrying under the mistaken belief that the install itself had failed.","confidence":"low"}],"next_attempt_guidance":["Run apt-get install in the background with output redirected to a log file and a PID file, then poll with short exec calls, instead of a single foreground call — this avoids hitting the platform's 120s proxy read timeout on all three nodes and prevents wasted retries/investigation.","Where nodes have no dependency on each other (e.g. installing postgresql, copying the CA bundle, writing conf.d/tls_repl.conf), issue the exec calls for node1/node2/node3 back-to-back without waiting on each one sequentially, to reduce total wall-clock time within the fixed session budget.","After TLS material and conf.d changes are in place, immediately add explicit hostssl pg_hba.conf rules for the replication database requiring clientcert=verify-full tied to the new CA (e.g. `hostssl replication replicator 0.0.0.0/0 cert clientcert=verify-full`) before moving on — do this early rather than leaving it for a later pass, since it is central to the task requirement of accepting replication only from CA-issued certs.","Verify actual package/service state with idempotent checks (e.g. `dpkg -l | grep postgresql-16`, `systemctl is-active postgresql`) rather than trusting a tool-reported timeout as a functional failure, to avoid unnecessary re-work.","Reserve time explicitly for the replica bootstrap phase (pg_basebackup from node1, standby.signal or primary_conninfo with sslmode=verify-full and sslcert/sslkey pointing at the replicator certificate, postgresql restart) since this phase had not started by the time of the last observed evidence — consider doing a time-budget check partway through the session and prioritizing this phase if time is short.","Do not assume the old cluster's partial state is reusable; start fresh and re-run the full sequence (CA creation, cert distribution, postgresql.conf/pg_hba.conf edits, restart, replica bootstrap, verification with `SELECT * FROM pg_stat_replication` on node1) end-to-end on the new cluster."]},"ttft_ms":16029,"type":"result","duration_ms":53261,"uuid":"8e222df5-77cd-440f-8a2b-1f6ea7b7b481","ttft_stream_ms":2101,"time_to_request_ms":40,"first_content_frame_ms":2897,"queued_turn_count":0}
